# Grid Energy Ecosystem — Technical Design & Simulation Specification v1.0

**Status:** Design-only. No implementation code included, per project requirement.
**Labeling convention used throughout:** `[PHYSICS-BASED]`, `[GAME APPROXIMATION]`, `[CONFIGURABLE]`.

---

## SECTION 1 — Game Objective

The player (human or AI agent) is given a procedurally-defined grid terrain. They place energy-generation
infrastructure (solar, wind, hydro) and connective infrastructure (cable) to generate electricity, deliver it
to demand zones, and earn revenue, while the simulated environment (sun, wind, clouds, water cycle, seasons)
evolves over simulated time — up to 10 in-game years. The player wins by building an ecosystem that remains
economically and energetically viable under changing and eventually unfamiliar conditions.

## SECTION 2 — Research Objective

The game is an evaluation harness for AI world-model formation. It measures whether an agent can:
spatial-reason over terrain/wind/water geometry; plan construction sequences; remember prior environmental
outcomes; learn the *causal* rules (not surface correlations like "sand→solar"); predict consequences of
actions and events; adapt to seasonal/long-horizon change; and transfer learned rules to unfamiliar map
layouts it has never seen. Section 39/40 define the formal evaluation protocol.

## SECTION 3 — World Model

The world is decomposed into five strictly separated layers (never merged in state or in code):

- **A. Static Terrain Properties** — fixed at map generation (elevation, roughness, permeability, etc.)
- **B. Dynamic Environmental State** — evolves every tick (wind, cloud, rain, river flow, snowpack, sun position)
- **C. Player-Placed Objects** — machines and cables, with their own static+dynamic sub-state
- **D. Derived Physical Values** — computed each tick from A+B+C (effective irradiance, local wind, hydraulic head, outputs)
- **E. Economic Values** — computed from D (revenue, cost, profit)

No rule may read E to compute D, or D to compute A/B (no backward causation). This ordering is enforced by
the Update Order in Section 24/31.

## SECTION 4 — Coordinate / Grid System

- Grid: 2D array, coordinates `(x, y)`, origin at top-left, `x` increasing east, `y` increasing south. `[GAME APPROXIMATION]`
- Cell size: 1 abstract unit (≈ configurable to represent e.g. 100m). `[CONFIGURABLE]`
- Each cell holds exactly one **base terrain block**, zero-or-more **overlays** (stacked, ordered), and
  zero-or-one **machine**.
- Neighbor model: 8-connected (N, S, E, W, NE, NW, SE, SW) for wind/cloud propagation and orographic effects;
  4-connected (N, S, E, W) for river flow directionality to keep flow routing unambiguous. `[GAME APPROXIMATION]`
- Global wind direction is a compass bearing (0–359°); "windward"/"leeward" for a cell are computed relative
  to that bearing and the elevation gradient of its neighbors (Section 8).

## SECTION 5 — Static Terrain Property System

Every block carries this fixed property set (values never change after map generation, only reinforcement
overlays modify *effective* stability/permeability, tracked separately as derived state):

`elevation`, `roughnessZ0` (m), `moistureCapacity` (0–1), `permeability` (0–1), `stability` (0–1),
`thermalInertia` (relative, only where relevant), `baseWaterRetention` (0–1), `allowedOverlays[]`,
`forbiddenOverlays[]`, `allowedMachines[]`, `transformable` (bool), `reinforceable` (bool).

These feed the Wind, Water Cycle, and Placement engines. They never directly set an energy multiplier.

