### 6.1 Grass/Dirt
Baseline terrain, moderate infiltration, moderate roughness. No inherent energy bonus. Neighbors: passes wind
speed through with roughness-based damping (Section 7). Seasonally: retains moderate moisture in all seasons;
in winter, moisture below freezing threshold contributes to local snow accumulation eligibility.

### 6.2 Sand
Low roughness → low wind drag, allows wind to accelerate slightly across it (Section 7, RULE-WIND-002). High
infiltration → rainfall mostly infiltrates rather than running off (little contribution to river flow). Its
solar output is *never* set directly — it emerges purely from the Sun Model (Section 6/9) because sand rarely
casts obstruction shadows (low elevation-neighbor variance) and rarely retains cloud-forming moisture.

### 6.3 Mud/Clay
Unstable (`stability = 0.30`) because saturated fine-grained soil has low bearing capacity `[PHYSICS-BASED,
simplified]`. Solar/Wind machines require `stability ≥ 0.7` (Section 15), so Mud needs a Gravel or Stone
overlay (reinforcement) to raise *effective* stability before those machines are legal. High moisture capacity
and low permeability mean high evaporation contribution feeding the Cloud system, and high runoff generation
when saturated (once `moistureCapacity` is exceeded, excess becomes runoff rather than infiltration — RULE-
WATER-004). Under sustained high rainfall + adjacency to a Water block, Mud can transform into Water
(RULE-TERRAIN-003) once accumulated surface water exceeds a threshold `[CONFIGURABLE]`.

### 6.4 Stone/Cobblestone/Rock
High elevation, high roughness. Never given a flat wind multiplier — instead its elevation changes exposure
to the ambient wind field, and its roughness dampens the *local* wind speed component from friction (these two
effects partially oppose and the net direction is computed, not asserted; see Section 7 equations). Very
stable, very low permeability → high runoff contribution, essentially no infiltration.

### 6.5 Snow/Peak
Highest elevation. Contributes to orographic lifting (Section 8). Only Wind machines are allowed (Solar is
blocked by permanent low sun-angle incidence + high albedo scattering `[GAME APPROXIMATION]`; Hydro/Cable
placement is invalid on frozen ground). Snow accumulates from precipitation when local temperature ≤ 0°C
and melts when local temperature > 0°C, releasing water into runoff/infiltration proportionally (Section 13).

### 6.6 Water/River
Distinguishes `RIVER` (directional flow, `flowRate Q > 0`), `LAKE` (large stagnant body), and `STAGNANT_WATER`
(small isolated body, e.g., pond). Only `LAKE` and `STAGNANT_WATER`, or `RIVER` cells below a velocity
threshold `velocity ≤ V_float_max [CONFIGURABLE]`, permit Floating Solar (RULE-PLACE-006) — fast rivers would
destroy or de-stabilize a floating platform `[GAME APPROXIMATION]`.

### 6.7 Gravel
Mid-elevation transition/reinforcement terrain. Its primary role is as an **overlay** that raises the
effective `stability` and `permeability` of the cell beneath it (RULE-OVERLAY-001), enabling Mud or Sand to
later host Solar/Wind after being reinforced.

## SECTION 7 — Cloud System

State per cloud entity: `cloudDensity` (0–1), `cloudWaterContent` (kg/m² abstracted), `cloudTemperature`,
`cloudAltitude`, `movementDirection`, `movementSpeed`, `precipitationPotential`, `cloudLifetime`,
`coverageRadius`, centered on a grid position (clouds are objects that move across cells, not a per-cell
scalar field, so that "cloud crossing the map" is a legible, trackable spatial event).

**Formation** `[GAME APPROXIMATION of real convective/orographic cloud physics]`:
`evaporation(cell) + humidity(cell) ≥ H_condensation_threshold [CONFIGURABLE]` AND
`(temperature(cell) ≤ dewPointProxy(cell) OR orographicLift(cell) > 0)` → spawn/grow a cloud entity centered
on that cell with `cloudDensity` proportional to excess humidity above threshold.

**Movement:** cloud position advances each tick by `movementDirection/movementSpeed`, which is derived from
the ambient wind vector at the cloud's current altitude-adjusted position (higher altitude → less terrain
drag, more purely wind-driven) `[GAME APPROXIMATION]`.

**Dissipation:** `cloudDensity` decays by a configurable rate per tick, faster over dry, low-humidity, hot
cells (re-evaporation of cloud droplets), and instantaneously if the cloud has fully precipitated
(`cloudWaterContent → 0`).

**Precipitation:** each tick, `precipitation = cloudWaterContent × precipitationPotential × f(orographicLift)`,
where `precipitationPotential` rises with `cloudDensity` and with forced lifting over high terrain
(Section 8). Precipitated water is removed from `cloudWaterContent` and added to the underlying cell's
`rainfall` for that tick, feeding the Water Cycle (Section 9).

**Effect on solar:** `cloudAttenuation(cell) = 1 − cloudDensity(overhead cloud) × k_atten [CONFIGURABLE]`,
multiplied directly into `effectiveSolarIrradiance` (Section 6 solar chain) — never a flat terrain penalty.

## SECTION 8 — Sun / Solar Model

Variables: `timeOfDay`, `dayOfYear`, `season`, `sunAzimuth`, `sunElevation`, `baseSolarIrradiance`,
`cloudDensity`, `terrainObstruction`, `panelOrientation`, `panelTilt`.

`sunElevation(t) = f(dayOfYear, timeOfDay, latitudeConst)` — a smooth sinusoidal approximation
`[GAME APPROXIMATION]`: peaks at solar noon, is 0 at sunrise/sunset, amplitude modulated seasonally
(higher peak in summer, lower in winter, per Section 19 season table).

`baseSolarIrradiance = I_max × max(0, sin(sunElevation))` `[PHYSICS-BASED, simplified Lambertian model]`.

`terrainObstruction(cell)`: 1.0 (no shadow) minus a shadow contribution from any higher-elevation neighbor
cell lying in the direction of `sunAzimuth` within a shadow-casting range proportional to elevation
difference `[GAME APPROXIMATION deterministic ray-cast on the elevation grid]`.

`panelIncidenceFactor = cos(angle between sun-vector and panel-normal)`, computed from `panelTilt` /
`panelOrientation` vs. `sunAzimuth`/`sunElevation` `[PHYSICS-BASED]`.

`effectiveSolarIrradiance = baseSolarIrradiance × terrainObstruction × cloudAttenuation × panelIncidenceFactor`

`solarOutput = effectiveSolarIrradiance × panelArea × panelEfficiency` `[PHYSICS-BASED, simplified]`.

Level 1 uses only `baseSolarIrradiance` with panel flat/fixed (no orientation, no obstruction) to introduce
the concept gently; obstruction and orientation are introduced at Levels 3–4 (Section 20).

## SECTION 9 — Wind System

`globalWind = (speed, direction)`, set by the weather/season layer, seeded and bounded (Section 29).

`localWind(cell) = globalWind` modified by:
1. **Roughness damping:** `speedFactor = (z0_ref / z0_cell)^α` — smaller `z0` (Sand) → less damping → wind
   accelerates slightly; larger `z0` (Stone/Snow) → more damping `[PHYSICS-BASED, simplified log-wind-profile idea]`.
2. **Elevation exposure:** each +1 elevation over the map-average adds a small speed bonus (more exposed to
   free-stream wind) `[GAME APPROXIMATION]`.
3. **Orographic wind-shadow:** if a higher-elevation cell sits upwind within `shadowRange [CONFIGURABLE]`,
   apply a leeward speed reduction proportional to the elevation difference and inversely to distance
   (Section 8/RULE-WIND-003).
4. **Directional deflection near mountains:** wind direction bends slightly around large elevation gradients
   `[GAME APPROXIMATION, optional — mark CONFIGURABLE for whether Level ≥7 enables this]`.

`localWind(cell)` is a *vector*, always exposed as ground truth (Section 28) even where the agent's
observation is restricted (Section 21).

## SECTION 10 — Mountain / Orographic System

Chain 1 (wind side): `windward slope → forced ascent → adiabatic cooling → increases local humidity
saturation → cloud formation bias` (feeds Section 7 formation threshold). `leeward slope → descending,
warming, drying air → wind shadow (Section 9.3) → suppressed cloud formation ("rain shadow")`
`[PHYSICS-BASED, simplified orographic-lift model]`.

`orographicLift(cell) = max(0, elevation(cell) − elevation(upwind neighbor)) × windSpeedFactor`. This feeds
directly into the Cloud formation formula (Section 7) and into `precipitationPotential`.

**Range of influence:** a mountain cell's wind-shadow and lift effects propagate up to `R_orographic`
cells downwind/upwind `[CONFIGURABLE, default 3–5]`, decaying linearly with distance.

**Combining multiple mountain cells:** effects are additive but capped (`min(1.0, Σ contributions)`) to avoid
runaway values from long contiguous ranges — a ridge does not produce infinite lift.

**Valleys:** a valley (local elevation minimum flanked by two higher neighbors along the wind axis) receives
reduced wind speed (channeling only along its axis, damping cross-axis) `[GAME APPROXIMATION]` and is a
collection point for both runoff (Section 9 water cycle) and — separately — for any accumulated cold air
(lower temperature, feeding snow/frost logic at low temperatures).

## SECTION 11 — Water Cycle

`evaporation(cell) = baseEvapRate × temperature_factor × (1 − humidity) × surfaceWaterAvailable(cell)`
`[GAME APPROXIMATION]`. Feeds atmospheric humidity, which feeds Cloud formation (Section 7).

`infiltration(cell) = min(availableWater, permeability(cell) × infiltrationRateConst)`.

`runoff(cell) = availableWater − infiltration(cell) − evaporation(cell)`, routed downhill to the
lowest-elevation 4-connected neighbor (ties broken deterministically by fixed direction priority N>E>S>W to
preserve reproducibility) — this is the River Flow Model's water source term (Section 12).

Terrain differentiation (all derived from the static permeability/retention properties in Section 6, never
hardcoded per-rule again): Sand → high infiltration, low runoff; Mud → low infiltration, high retention until
saturated then high runoff; Rock → near-zero infiltration, high runoff; Grass → moderate/moderate.

## SECTION 12 — River Flow Model

Each Water cell tracks `waterLevel`, `flowRate Q`, `flowDirection`, `velocity`.

`flowDirection(cell) = argmin(waterLevel of 4-connected neighbor)` (deterministic, tie-broken as above).

`Q(cell) = Q(upstream contributing cells) + runoff(local contributing land cells) − evaporation(cell) −
infiltration(cell, if applicable to lake beds)`.

Source cells are Water cells with no upstream Water neighbor but receiving land runoff (springs). Tributaries
merge by summing `Q`. Downstream propagation is one full pass per tick in flow-direction topological order
(guaranteed acyclic because flow always goes toward lower `waterLevel`, and `waterLevel` is itself derived
each tick from the previous tick's state, preventing circularity — see Section 24).

`velocity = Q / (channelWidth × channelDepth)` `[PHYSICS-BASED, simplified continuity equation]`, with
`channelWidth`/`channelDepth` set at map generation per Water cell `[CONFIGURABLE]`.

`STAGNANT_WATER`/`LAKE` cells: `flowDirection = none` unless overflow threshold exceeded, `velocity ≈ 0`.

Overflow: if `waterLevel` exceeds a cell's basin capacity, excess is redistributed to the lowest neighbor as
additional runoff next tick (bounded, prevents infinite accumulation).

## SECTION 13 — Snow System

`snowAccumulation(cell) += precipitation(cell)` when `temperature(cell) ≤ 0°C`, else precipitation falls as
rain (adds to `rainfall` directly).

`snowmelt(cell) = min(snowDepth(cell), meltRateConst × max(0, temperature(cell)))` `[GAME APPROXIMATION,
degree-day melt model — a standard, simple, well-established hydrology approximation]`.

Melted snow is added to `availableWater(cell)` for that tick, entering infiltration/runoff exactly like
rainfall (Section 11). Sustained melt in Spring is therefore a predictable, learnable spike in downstream
river flow — a designed causal chain for the Rule Learning / Causal Reasoning tests (Sections 23–24).

## SECTION 14 — Machine System

### Land Solar
`capacity`, `efficiency`, `orientation`, `tilt`, `area`, `maintenanceCost`, `lifespanYears`, `buildCost`.
Output per Section 8's `solarOutput` equation.

### Floating Solar
Same core properties plus `waterCompatibility` flag and optional `coolingBonusFactor
[CONFIGURABLE, small, physically-motivated: cooler cell temperature raises panel efficiency slightly —
PHYSICS-BASED but magnitude is a tunable, documented constant, not a hidden multiplier]`.

### Wind Turbine
`ratedPower`, `rotorSweptArea`, `efficiency (Betz-limited constant ≤ 0.593)`, `orientation/yaw`, `cutIn`,
`ratedSpeed`, `cutOut`, `buildCost`, `maintenanceCost`, `lifespanYears`.

`relativeAngle = |windDirection − turbineOrientation|`; `orientationEfficiency = cos(relativeAngle)` clipped
to ≥ 0 `[PHYSICS-BASED, simplified yaw-misalignment cosine loss]`.

`windPowerAvailable = 0.5 × airDensity × rotorSweptArea × windSpeed³` `[PHYSICS-BASED]`.

`windOutput = 0` if `windSpeed < cutIn` or `> cutOut`; otherwise
`= min(ratedPower, windPowerAvailable × efficiency × orientationEfficiency)`, i.e. output saturates at
`ratedPower` once wind reaches `ratedSpeed` (standard turbine power curve) `[GAME APPROXIMATION of the real
curve's smooth knee — modeled here as a clipped cubic for simplicity]`.

### Hydro Turbine
`P = ρ × g × Q × H × efficiency` `[PHYSICS-BASED]` — see Section 15 for full breakdown.

### Cable
`capacity` (max throughput), `transmissionLossPerCell [CONFIGURABLE]`, `buildCost`, `maintenanceCost`.

## SECTION 15 — Machine Placement Rules

`CAN_PLACE(machine, cell, worldState) → VALID | INVALID(reason)`.

Checks performed, in order (fail-fast, first failing check is the returned reason):
1. Terrain compatibility: `machine.type ∈ terrain.allowedMachines`.
2. Overlay compatibility: current overlay stack (if any) does not forbid `machine.type`.
3. Effective stability ≥ machine's `minStability` (Solar/Wind require `≥ 0.7 [CONFIGURABLE]`; effective
   stability = base `stability` + reinforcement bonus from Gravel/Stone overlays, capped at 1.0).
4. Water compatibility (Hydro requires a Water cell with `flowRate > Q_min` OR sufficient `waterLevel` for a
   lake installation; Floating Solar requires `velocity ≤ V_float_max`).
5. Slope/clearance: no machine on a cell whose elevation differs from all placeable neighbors by more than a
   max-slope constant `[CONFIGURABLE]` (prevents absurd cliff-edge placements).
6. Machine-specific requirements (e.g., Wind Turbine requires no taller machine within its rotor-diameter
   radius, to avoid wake shadowing another turbine — `[GAME APPROXIMATION]`).
7. Neighbor restrictions (e.g., two Hydro turbines cannot occupy the same contiguous water segment above a
   density cap, representing shared head/flow competition).
8. Connection requirement (if the level enforces grid connection — Section 16): a Cable path to a
   substation/demand zone must exist or be plannable.

Example: `INVALID: "Wind turbine requires stability >= 0.7, cell has 0.30 (unreinforced Mud)"`.

## SECTION 16 — Overlay Rules

Stack order (bottom→top): `BASE TERRAIN → TERRAIN OVERLAY → INFRASTRUCTURE/REINFORCEMENT → MACHINE → CABLE`.

Key combinations:
- Mud + Gravel = reinforced Mud (`stability += ΔG [CONFIGURABLE]`) → may then host Cable, and after a second
  reinforcement tier (+Stone) may host Solar/Wind if the resulting effective stability clears 0.7.
- Water + Floating Solar = valid only if `velocity ≤ V_float_max` (Section 6.6).
- Sand + Land Solar = valid (base stability of Sand, `0.55`, is `[CONFIGURABLE]` — if the design target keeps
  Sand below 0.7, Sand needs a Gravel reinforcement too; this is intentionally left tunable rather than
  silently allowing Sand to trivially host Solar, to avoid the "sand=solar" memorization trap called out in
  Section 41).
- Snow + Land Solar = invalid (terrain forbids Solar outright, Section 6 Snow entry).
- Stone + Wind = valid (Stone stability 0.95 clears threshold).
- Full compatibility matrices: Section 36 / Final Overlay Compatibility Matrix.

## SECTION 17 — Energy Network

Machines generate energy each tick → energy enters connected Cable network → flows toward the nearest
demand zone/substation → `transmissionLoss = distance(cells) × transmissionLossPerCell` is subtracted →
delivered energy compared against zone demand → surplus (if any) is either curtailed (Levels 1–8, no storage)
or, if a level unlocks storage `[CONFIGURABLE, later levels only]`, banked up to a capacity limit.

Grid-connection requirement is itself level-gated (Section 20): early levels allow direct machine→zone
delivery without cables to teach core mechanics; Level ≥6 requires an unbroken Cable path.

## SECTION 18 — Economic Model

`revenue(tick) = deliveredEnergy(zone) × price(zone, demandLevel)` `[GAME APPROXIMATION — price is not a
free market, it is a configurable schedule per demand tier]`.

Costs: `buildCost` (one-time), `maintenanceCost` (recurring per tick/day), `transmissionLoss` (implicit
revenue loss), `machineDegradation` (health decays with age/weather exposure, reducing efficiency and
eventually output to 0 at end of `lifespanYears`), `replacementCost` (rebuild after failure/end-of-life),
`weatherDowntime` (e.g., extreme wind above `cutOut` forces turbine shutdown; extreme flood may damage a
low-lying machine — `[CONFIGURABLE]` failure probabilities, always seeded).

`profit(tick) = revenue(tick) − maintenanceCost(tick)`; `netWorth` accumulates profit minus one-time
build/replacement costs.

## SECTION 19 — Time System

Hierarchy: `Tick → Hour → Day → Season → Year`. `[CONFIGURABLE]` ticks/hour and hours/day, default suggestion:
1 tick = 1 simulated hour, 24 ticks = 1 day, ~90 days = 1 season, 360 days = 1 year (round numbers for
learnability). Time acceleration lets the player fast-forward; the underlying engine still computes every
tick internally for determinism (no skipped physics).

Update cadence:
- **Fast (every tick):** sun position, wind, cloud movement/formation/precipitation.
- **Medium (every tick, but visibly slower-changing):** evaporation, infiltration, runoff, river flow, snow.
- **Slow (daily/seasonal):** machine health/degradation, maintenance cost accrual, demand-zone level changes,
  season transition parameters.

## SECTION 20 — Season System

Seasons: Spring, Summer, Autumn, Winter. Each has a parameter table (see Final tables) adjusting: solar
amplitude/day-length (feeds Section 8 sinusoid), base temperature, base humidity/rainfall probability, global
wind speed distribution, snow accumulation vs. melt bias, and river baseflow (spring snowmelt surge; summer
lower baseflow; autumn rain increase; winter freeze reduces flow). None of these are applied as a flat energy
multiplier — each modifies an upstream environmental variable that then propagates through the existing
equations (Sections 8–13).

## SECTION 21 — Level 1–10 Progression

| Level | Terrain/Blocks | Machines | New Rules Introduced | Objective | Key Reasoning Skill |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Grass, Sand only | Land Solar | Flat sun model, no shadows/orientation | Reach a generation target | Basic placement |
| 2 | + Stone, Gravel | + Wind Turbine | Static wind field, roughness/elevation effects | Compare Solar vs Wind under fixed conditions | Comparative tradeoff reasoning |
| 3 | Same | Same | Wind direction + turbine orientation matters | Orient turbines correctly | Directional reasoning |
| 4 | Same | Same | Sun azimuth/elevation + season intro, panel orientation | Track daily/seasonal solar variation | Temporal reasoning |
| 5 | + Water/River | + Hydro Turbine | Flow/head model | Add hydro to the portfolio | New-modality integration |
| 6 | + Cloud overlays | Same | Full water cycle, rain affects river flow, cloud affects solar; Cable/grid connection required | Manage interacting subsystems | Systemic/causal reasoning |
| 7 | + Mountain ranges | Same | Wind shadow, orographic lift | Exploit/avoid windward vs leeward zones | Spatial + causal reasoning |
| 8 | + Snow/Peak | Same | Orographic rainfall, snowmelt-driven river surges | Plan around seasonal hydro variability | Delayed-consequence / temporal-causal reasoning |
| 9 | Full terrain set, multiple demand zones | + Cable network at scale | Demand-zone economics, transmission loss | Build an efficient, profitable multi-source grid | Resource allocation / optimization |
