## SECTION 23 — Equations Index (summary; full derivations inline above)

| # | Relationship | Equation | Label |
|---|---|---|---|
| E1 | Solar irradiance | `I = I_max × max(0, sin(sunElevation))` | PHYSICS-BASED (simplified) |
| E2 | Effective irradiance | `I_eff = I × obstruction × cloudAtten × cosIncidence` | PHYSICS-BASED |
| E3 | Solar output | `P_solar = I_eff × area × efficiency` | PHYSICS-BASED (simplified) |
| E4 | Wind power available | `P_wind_avail = 0.5 ρ A v³` | PHYSICS-BASED |
| E5 | Turbine output | `P_wind = clip(P_wind_avail × η × cos(Δangle), 0, ratedPower)` for `v∈[cutIn,cutOut]` else 0 | GAME APPROXIMATION |
| E6 | Local wind speed | `v_local = v_global × roughnessFactor(z0) × elevationFactor × windShadowFactor` | GAME APPROXIMATION |
| E7 | Evaporation | `E = k_evap × T_factor × (1−humidity) × waterAvail` | GAME APPROXIMATION |
| E8 | Cloud formation trigger | `evap+humidity ≥ threshold AND (cooling OR lift)` | GAME APPROXIMATION |
| E9 | Precipitation | `rain = cloudWaterContent × precipPotential × f(lift)` | GAME APPROXIMATION |
| E10 | Infiltration | `I_f = min(avail, permeability × k_inf)` | GAME APPROXIMATION |
| E11 | Runoff | `R = avail − I_f − E` | GAME APPROXIMATION (mass-balance identity) |
| E12 | Snow accumulation | `snow += precip` if `T≤0` | GAME APPROXIMATION |
| E13 | Snowmelt | `melt = min(snow, k_melt × max(0,T))` | GAME APPROXIMATION (degree-day) |
| E14 | River flow | `Q_down = ΣQ_up + R_local − E − I_f(lakebed)` | GAME APPROXIMATION (mass-balance) |
| E15 | Velocity | `v = Q / (width × depth)` | PHYSICS-BASED (continuity) |
| E16 | Hydraulic head | `H = waterLevel(upstream) − waterLevel(turbine cell)` | PHYSICS-BASED (simplified) |
| E17 | Hydro output | `P_hydro = ρ g Q H η`, 0 if `Q<Q_min` or `H<H_min` | PHYSICS-BASED |
| E18 | Transmission | `delivered = generated − distance×lossPerCell` | GAME APPROXIMATION |
| E19 | Revenue | `rev = delivered × price(zone,tier)` | GAME APPROXIMATION |

Variables, units, ranges, and worked examples for each equation are given at first use in Sections 6–18
above; the Example Walkthrough (Section 39) shows concrete numbers end-to-end.

## SECTION 24 — Simulation Update Order (per tick)

1. Advance time (tick/hour/day/season/year counters)
2. Sun position (azimuth, elevation) from time+season
3. Seasonal parameter update (temperature baseline, humidity baseline, wind distribution)
4. Temperature update per cell (baseline + local modifiers, e.g. altitude lapse)
5. Global wind vector update (weather layer, seeded)
6. Local (terrain-modified) wind per cell (Section 9)
7. Evaporation per cell (needs temperature, humidity, water availability — all already current)
8. Cloud formation (needs evaporation, humidity, orographic lift from elevation+wind, all current)
9. Cloud movement (needs local wind, current)
10. Precipitation (needs cloud state just updated)
11. Snow accumulation/melt (needs precipitation + temperature, current)
12. Infiltration (needs precipitation+snowmelt as "available water", current)
13. Runoff (needs infiltration, current — mass balance closes here)
14. River flow update, source→downstream topological order (needs runoff, current)
15. Machine outputs: Solar (needs sun+cloud+obstruction, all current), Wind (needs local wind, current),
    Hydro (needs river flow/head, current)
16. Transmission (needs machine outputs, current)
17. Demand evaluation (zone schedule, independent input)
18. Revenue (needs delivered energy vs. demand, both current)
19. Machine health/degradation update (age, cumulative weather stress)
20. Record full state snapshot (ground truth log, Section 28)
21. Evaluate objectives / scoring deltas (Section 27)

**Why this order avoids circularity:** every step only reads state that was either (a) fixed before the tick
began (static terrain, Section 5) or (b) already recomputed earlier in *this same* tick, never a
value that this tick has not yet produced. Rivers, the deepest dependency chain, are updated last among the
physical layers, after evaporation/precipitation/infiltration have already been resolved for this tick, so
`Q` never depends on a not-yet-updated future value. Economic values (E) are computed strictly after all
physical/derived values (D), consistent with the layering in Section 3.

